From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 22:22:43 +0200
Subject: nv30: on the PS3, every index binding keeps within the kernel's
 share, and a binding with its BEGIN, or a piece whole, never spans two
 submissions

The review of 0072 found two ways an enforcing ps3rsx still refuses a submission, losing its draws:

- Only a split draw's pieces checked the file's share of the kernel's index ring (1.25 MiB of
  copies a submission). Plain indexed draws after a split draw's last piece, or after a kick inside
  a primitive (which leaves the kernel's 1 MiB tail copy counted), could add about 230 KiB more and
  get the submission refused. Now every index binding on the GPU path checks it first and goes in
  a submission of its own when it would pass (nv30_idx_binding_room). Mesa counts every draw as a
  fresh copy, so this also keeps each submission's scan under the kernel's.
- A push-buffer flush could fall between a binding and its BEGIN (the batches then go through half
  a binding, refused; there since 1493 for every indexed draw), or inside a piece (the kernel then
  copies on to the buffer's end, past what the share counted). Now a plain draw reserves the
  binding and its BEGIN together, and a piece reserves all of its words (binding, BEGIN, batches,
  END) before its binding, so a flush falls only before them.

A kick for the share that the kernel cancels gives up the rest of the draw (its state is gone; the
next validate sends it again). NV30_PS3_IDX_SPLIT=0 prints a line, as the other placement
switches do; the cancelled-submission line says the draws were lost and that NV30_PS3_IDX_STATS
has the split counts. Draws under the cap send the same words as before 0072.

Forwarded: no

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nv30/nv30_screen.c b/src/gallium/drivers/nouveau/nv30/nv30_screen.c
